<p>Owners, managers and accountants see the <strong>Dashboard</strong> and <strong>Reports</strong>. Other roles don't.</p>

<h2>The owner's dashboard</h2>
<p>The dashboard is the first thing owners and managers see. It shows today's takings against the same day last week, the number of sales
  and the average sale, gross margin, cash in the drawers, takings by hour and over the last week, best sellers, each branch, money owed both
  ways, low stock, orders ready and deliveries due. It updates by itself as sales happen, so it can stay open on the office screen. Warnings,
  such as sales KRA refused, appear at the top.</p>

<h2>Reports</h2>
<p>Open <strong>Reports</strong>, choose a report, then a period (today, this week, last month, or your own dates) and a branch. Every report
  can be viewed on screen, downloaded as <strong>CSV</strong> to open in Excel, or as a <strong>PDF</strong> on your letterhead.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Sales and margin</strong>: takings, returns, tax, cost and margin by day, month, branch, cashier, category or product.</li>
  <li><strong>Profit and loss</strong>: sales less returns, cost of sales, gross profit, stock losses and till payouts.</li>
  <li><strong>Tax (VAT)</strong>: output tax by rate, input tax from supplier invoices, and VAT payable, for your return.</li>
  <li><strong>Payments by method</strong> (Airtel Money appears once it's connected or used), <strong>M-Pesa reconciliation</strong>, and <strong>Shifts and cash counts</strong> with each
    shift's over or short.</li>
  <li><strong>Discounts, voids and returns</strong>: per cashier, with who approved the big ones.</li>
  <li><strong>Stock valuation</strong> at cost and selling price, and <strong>Dead stock</strong>: what hasn't sold for a while and the
    money tied up in it.</li>
  <li>Plus <strong>Who owes us</strong>, <strong>What we owe</strong> and the stock history.</li>
</ul>
<p>Margins are worked out from what each item cost on the day it was sold, so later price changes don't rewrite the past.</p>

<%= render "help/walkthrough", tour: HelpTour.find("report") %>

<h2>QuickBooks Online</h2>
<p><strong>Reports › QuickBooks export</strong> downloads a month of the books as a zip your accountant imports into
  QuickBooks Online: journal entries for each day's sales by branch, returns, voids, deposits, customer and supplier payments and supplier
  invoices, plus customer and supplier lists. Set the QuickBooks account names once on that page so they match your chart of accounts. Each
  journal file stays under QuickBooks' 1,000-line limit, and anything that couldn't be exported (a sale whose payments don't add up, say) is
  listed in <code>not-in-the-journal.csv</code> with the reason. See <%= link_to "QuickBooks Online export", help_article_path("quickbooks"), class: "link" %>.</p>

<h2>Emails to the owner</h2>
<p>Owners, managers and accountants can have the figures come to them, each turned on or off under <strong>My profile</strong>:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Daily summary</strong>, each morning at about 7:15: yesterday's takings with charts of the day by hour and the last seven days,
    branches, how it was paid and the best sellers. Then <strong>Worth a look</strong>: products at zero (which the till can't sell),
    what customers asked for that wasn't on the shelf, stock written off, cash counts that didn't balance, discounts, voids and returns
    by cashier, anything sold below cost, offline sales to check, tills left open and M-Pesa payments not on any sale. A day without
    sales sends nothing.</li>
  <li><strong>Weekly summary</strong>, each Monday: the week's takings against the eight before, each day, the best sellers and what they
    made over cost, who owes you and how late, supplier bills due this week, and stock that hasn't sold in 90 days.</li>
  <li><strong>Alerts</strong>, for owners and managers, sent straight away: a till closing short, a completed sale voided, or an approved
    stock count losing more than the amounts set on <strong>Settings › Shop details</strong>. Whoever did it isn't sent one.</li>
</ul>
